-- title:
--   Vanishing of U_q in the local Hecke algebra when q² ‖ N
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ and a set $S_0$ of natural numbers, and let $\mathcal{O}$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero (a domain, complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal) with residue field $k =$ `ResidueField 𝒪`. Assume, as a `Fact`, that [`CuspForm.HasIntegralStructure N 2`](def/CuspForm_IntegralStructure.html#L6) holds: the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule generated by the weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ all of whose $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers is the whole space. Let $\theta'$ be a ring homomorphism from [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S₀`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) — the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}$ of weight-two cusp forms on $\Gamma_0(N)$ generated by the operators `heckeTLin` for primes $\ell \nmid N$ with $\ell \notin S_0$ together with the operators `heckeULin` for primes $r \mid N$ with $r \notin S_0$ — to $k$. Let $q$ be a prime with $q \mid N$, $q \notin S_0$, $q^2 \mid N$ and $q^3 \nmid N$, suppose $q$ is a unit in $\mathcal{O}$, and suppose $\theta'(U_q) = 0$, where $U_q =$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra.U hq hqN hqS₀`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L80) is the element of the Hecke algebra given by `heckeULin 2 hqN`. Then the image of $U_q$ under [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.π N S₀ 𝒪 θ'`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L151), the canonical map of the Hecke algebra into [`CuspForm.heckeLocal N S₀ 𝒪 θ'`](def/CuspForm_HeckeLocal.html#L131) (the localisation of `heckeBaseAlgebra N S₀ 𝒪` at the multiplicative set obtained by transporting, along `latticeBaseChange`, the complement of the prime ideal `heckeCharKernel N S₀ 𝒪 θ'` attached to $\theta'$), is zero.
--
--   This is the Atkin–Lehner-type statement that at a prime $q$ exactly dividing the level to the second power, and prime to the residue characteristic, the operator $U_q$ becomes nilpotent-to-zero in the local factor of the Hecke algebra cut out by a character killing $U_q$; it is the step used in the level-lowering and modularity-lifting analysis of the local Hecke algebra. It is cited in the study of `heckeLocal`, notably in the results on bijectivity, on representing elements by $\pi(T_\ell)$ for $\ell \nmid N$, and on reducedness under the Frobenius characteristic-polynomial hypothesis.
-- source:
